  {
    "pkattachmentId": 49713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "chapterId": 314,
    "fileId": 18591,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Follicular adenoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "This example demonstrates a thin capsule that imparts separation from the surrounding parenchyma. It is composed of a mixture of microfollicles and macrofollicles, and it is overall more cellular and distinct from the surrounding thyroid.",
    "source": "Raja Seethala",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/314/18591.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/314/thumb_18591.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 49714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "chapterId": 314,
    "fileId": 18592,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Follicular adenoma",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "The nuclei are bland and monomorphic and have round to ovoid nucleoli with condensed chromatin. The nuclei should not deviate much from those in the adjacent normal tissue, seen here as a compressed follicle.",
    "source": "Raja Seethala",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/314/18592.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/314/thumb_18592.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 49715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "chapterId": 314,
    "fileId": 18593,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Follicular adenoma with papillary architecture",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Like conventional follicular adenomas, those with papillary architecture are demarcated from the surrounding thyroid by a capsule but often show cystic change and a pseudopapillary architecture, reminiscent of a hyperplastic nodule. These are fairly common in adolescents.",
    "source": "Raja Seethala",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/314/18593.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/314/thumb_18593.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 49716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "chapterId": 314,
    "fileId": 18594,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Follicular adenoma (FA) with papillary architecture",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "The pseudopapillae seen in FA with papillary architecture show cuboidal to columnar cells with bland ovoid nuclei, similar to those seen in typical FA, and are polarized towards the basement membrane without much stratification or overlap. In contrast to the fibrovascular cores of true papillae seen in conventional papillary thyroid carcinoma, these papillae often show follicles within. The cytoplasm is often somewhat oncocytic. Hyperfunctioning adenomas may show some overlap with FA with papillary architecture but often show more vacuolated cytoplasm and scalloped colloid.",
    "source": "Raja Seethala",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/314/18594.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/314/thumb_18594.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 49718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "chapterId": 314,
    "fileId": 18596,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Follicular adenoma in a background of thyroiditis",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "This example shows a prominent microfollicular pattern and is notably spared by the lymphocytic infiltrates, which is typically the case for neoplasms in a thyroiditis background.",
    "source": "Raja Seethala",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/314/18596.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/44/314/thumb_18596.jpg"
  },
